The '''2011 UEFA Champions League final''' was an [[association football]] match played on 28 May 2011 at [[Wembley Stadium]] in London that decided the winner of the [[2010–11 UEFA Champions League|2010–11 season]] of the [[UEFA Champions League]]. The winners received the [[European Champion Clubs' Cup]] (the European Cup). The 2011 final was the culmination of the 56th season of the tournament, and the 19th in the Champions League era.

The final was contested by [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] of [[Royal Spanish Football Federation|Spain]] and [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] of [[The Football Association|England]], in a repeat of the [[2009 UEFA Champions League final|2009 final]] held in Rome, which Barcelona won 2–0. The match kicked off at 19:45 [[British Summer Time|BST]]. The [[Referee (association football)|referee]] for the match was [[Viktor Kassai]] from [[Hungarian Football Federation|Hungary]].<ref name="referee"/> The venue, the new Wembley Stadium, hosted its first European Cup final, having opened in 2007; the [[Wembley Stadium (1923)|old Wembley Stadium]] hosted the finals in [[1963 European Cup final|1963]], [[1968 European Cup final|1968]], [[1971 European Cup final|1971]], [[1978 European Cup final|1978]] and [[1992 European Cup final|1992]].<ref name="wembley">{{cite news|title=Wembley returns to centre stage |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/01d5-0e70c9e75e03-e82d6a4e7427-1000--wembley-returns-to-centre-stage/ |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=30 January 2009 |access-date=24 March 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100504123607/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid%3D795986.html |archive-date=4 May 2010 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Both teams entered the competition having won it three times previously; Manchester United in [[1967–68 European Cup|1968]], [[1998–99 UEFA Champions League|1999]] and [[2007–08 UEFA Champions League|2008]], and Barcelona in [[1991–92 European Cup|1992]], [[2005–06 UEFA Champions League|2006]] and [[2008–09 UEFA Champions League|2009]]. To reach the final, in the [[2010–11 UEFA Champions League knockout phase|knockout phase]] Barcelona beat [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]], [[FC Shakhtar Donetsk|Shakhtar Donetsk]] and lastly [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] in the 212th ''[[El Clásico]]'' derby, while Manchester United beat [[Olympique de Marseille|Marseille]], [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] and [[FC Schalke 04|Schalke 04]]. Both teams also entered the final as champions of their domestic leagues (the [[2010–11 Premier League|Premier League]] and [[2010–11 La Liga|La Liga]], respectively), but neither team had won a domestic cup that season.

Barcelona dominated the match, winning 3–1 with goals from [[Pedro (footballer, born 1987)|Pedro]], [[Lionel Messi]] and [[David Villa]], securing their fourth Champions League title.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/13576522.stm|title=Barcelona 3 Manchester United 1|date=28 May 2011|work=BBC Sport |access-date=30 May 2011| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20110529112634/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/13576522.stm| archive-date= 29 May 2011 | url-status=live}}</ref> [[Wayne Rooney]] scored for Manchester United to level the score going into half-time.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/champions-league/8542452/Barcelona-3-Manchester-United-1-match-report.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220112/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/champions-league/8542452/Barcelona-3-Manchester-United-1-match-report.html |archive-date=12 January 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live|title=Barcelona 3 Manchester United 1|date=28 May 2011|work=The Daily Telegraph|access-date=30 May 2011}}{{cbignore}}</ref>

As a result, Barcelona qualified to play against [[FC Porto|Porto]], the winners of the [[2010–11 UEFA Europa League]], in the [[2011 UEFA Super Cup]] in Monaco on 26 August 2011,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.dfb.de/index.php?id=510208 |title=Rahmenterminkalender 2011/2012|work=DFB.de |publisher=Deutscher Fussball-Bund |date=24 November 2010 |access-date=17 April 2011|language=de }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.lfp.fr/calendriers/2011_2012/calendrier_2011_2012.pdf |title=Calendrier Général des Compétitions 2011/2012|work=LFP.fr |publisher=Ligue de Football Professionnel |date=31 March 2011 |access-date=17 April 2011|language=fr | archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20110525095446/http://www.lfp.fr/calendriers/2011_2012/calendrier_2011_2012.pdf| archive-date= 25 May 2011 | url-status=live}}</ref> and they also earned a place in the semi-finals of the [[2011 FIFA Club World Cup]] in December 2011 as the [[UEFA]] representative.

==Background==
The match was a rematch of the final two years earlier, which Barcelona had won 2–0 in [[Rome]]. United and Barcelona had both won three European titles prior to the match. United had won three years earlier against [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]], [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] in 1999, and [[S.L. Benfica|Benfica]] in 1968. Barcelona had won their first title 19 years earlier, against [[UC Sampdoria|Sampdoria]] at Wembley Stadium. They then won their second title in 2006, beating [[Arsenal F.C|Arsenal]] 2–1 in [[Paris]]. The most recent title win for Barcelona was against United in 2009 and it was the most recent final for both of these teams. However, in 2008, United defeated Barcelona 1–0 on aggregate, at the semi-final stage, en route to claiming their third trophy. Barcelona were appearing in the final for the third time in six years while United were for the third time in four years.<ref>{{Cite web |last=UEFA.com |title=Finals {{!}} History {{!}} UEFA Champions League |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/history/winners/finals/ |access-date=2025-01-06 |website=[[UEFA]] |language=en}}</ref>

==Venue==
[[File:Wembley Stadium interior.jpg|thumb|Inside Wembley Stadium]]

[[Wembley Stadium]] was selected as the venue for the 2011 UEFA Champions League final at a meeting of the UEFA Executive Committee in [[Nyon]], [[Switzerland]], on 29 January 2009.<ref name="unveil">{{cite news|title=UEFA unveil 2011 and 2012 final venues |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/01d5-0e70c9e763c9-0b023fd4e3e9-1000--uefa-unveil-2011-and-2012-final-venues/ |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=29 January 2009 |access-date=23 March 2010 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110609011924/http://www.uefa.com/uefa/aboutuefa/organisation/executivecommittee/news/newsid%3D796145.html |archive-date=9 June 2011 }}</ref> Other stadia in contention to host the final included [[Allianz Arena]] in [[Munich]] and [[Berlin]]'s [[Olympic Stadium (Berlin)|Olympiastadion]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Wembley to host 2011 Euro final |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/europe/7858932.stm |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=29 January 2009 |access-date=29 January 2009 | archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20090130074945/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/europe/7858932.stm| archive-date= 30 January 2009 | url-status=live}}</ref> Allianz Arena instead hosted the [[2012 UEFA Champions League final|2012 final]].<ref name="unveil"/> The weekend set aside by UEFA for the 2011 Champions League final was originally scheduled by [[The Football League]] as the date for the [[Football League play-offs|Football League play-off finals]], which are traditionally held on the British May bank holiday weekend; however, due to UEFA's requirement that they be given exclusive use of the venue in the lead-up to the final, the play-off finals had to be relocated. Due to this breach of their contract with [[The Football Association]], The Football League demanded compensation.<ref>{{cite news |title=Football League seek compensation after Wembley is double-booked |url=http://www.timesonline.co.uk/tol/sport/football/football_league/article5796755.ece |work=Times Online |publisher=Times Newspapers |date=24 February 2009 |access-date=12 September 2009 }}{{dead link|date=September 2024|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref> On 21 January 2011, as part of a settlement agreement with the FA, the [[Football League One|League One]] and [[Football League Two|League Two]] play-off finals were moved to [[Old Trafford]], [[Manchester]], to be played respectively on 29 May and 28 May 2011; the [[Football League Championship|Championship]] play-off final was not rescheduled.<ref>{{cite news |title=Old Trafford to host League One and Two play-off finals |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/9369296.stm |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=21 January 2011 |access-date=21 January 2011 | archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20110125141045/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/9369296.stm| archive-date= 25 January 2011 | url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Play-Offs head North |url=http://www.football-league.co.uk/footballleaguenews/20110121/play-offs-head-north_2293334_2271869 |work=football-league.co.uk |publisher=The Football League |date=21 January 2011 |access-date=21 January 2011 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110124133042/http://www.football-league.co.uk/footballleaguenews/20110121/play-offs-head-north_2293334_2271869 |archive-date=24 January 2011 }}</ref> The [[Conference National]] play-off final was also moved to Manchester, to be played at the [[City of Manchester Stadium]] on 21 May 2011.<ref>{{cite news |title=Blue Square Bet Premier final moves to Manchester |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/teams/m/man_city/9031342.stm |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=24 September 2010 |access-date=21 January 2011 }}</ref>

The [[Wembley Stadium (1923)|original Wembley Stadium]] hosted five European Cup finals prior to 2011. The [[1968 European Cup final|1968]] and [[1978 European Cup final|1978]] finals were both won by English sides: [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] beat [[S.L. Benfica|Benfica]] 4–1 in 1968 and [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] defeated [[Club Brugge KV|Club Brugge]] 1–0 in 1978. Benfica also lost in the [[1963 European Cup final|1963 final]], beaten 2–1 by [[A.C. Milan]], while [[AFC Ajax|Ajax]] won the first of three consecutive European Cups at Wembley in [[1971 European Cup final|1971]], beating [[Panathinaikos F.C.|Panathinaikos]] 2–0. In the [[1992 European Cup final|1992 final]], Spanish club [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] defeated Italian side [[UC Sampdoria|Sampdoria]] 1–0 in the final match played as the European Cup prior to the following season's introduction of the current Champions League format.

First opened for the [[British Empire Exhibition]] in 1923, the stadium was originally known as the Empire Stadium. That year, it hosted its first [[FA Cup Final]], when almost 200,000 spectators attempted to watch [[1923 FA Cup Final|the match]] between [[Bolton Wanderers F.C.|Bolton Wanderers]] and [[West Ham United F.C.|West Ham United]]. Wembley played host to all of [[England national football team|England]]'s matches at the [[1966 FIFA World Cup]], including the 4–2 victory over [[Germany national football team|West Germany]] in the [[1966 FIFA World Cup final|final]], and at [[UEFA Euro 1996]]. The original stadium was closed in 2000 and demolished three years later, to be replaced by a 90,000-capacity arena, which opened in 2007.<ref name="wembley"/>

